# The M7 soak

`--soak` is the harness with everything on: four CPU players, a status line a
second, the stuck-screen watchdog, and a log line for every minigame module
entered and left. It is started once and left running.

```sh
g4 run --rtc dolphin --card ~/mp4-soak.raw --freshcard --soak --turns 10 \
       --clickstat --play ~/soak.play --turbo --log ~/soak.log
```

`soak.play` is `port/ref/movies/board-start.play`'s metronome — START through
the boot, then A for four frames out of every sixty-four — extended to four
million frames. With `--com4` no screen needs a *specific* button any more
(`instDll` dismisses its own instruction screen when all four players are CPU,
`instDll/main.c:294`), so the metronome alone walks every menu, every results
screen, and every board restart. There are no STARTs after the boot: a START
on the board opens the pause menu, which is what cost §15.3 three captures.

**Save-file handling.** The soak gets its own card image and `--freshcard`, so
the roulette starts from a known played set and a crashed run does not leave a
half-written save for the next one. Without `--card` the soak would write the
same `~/Library/Application Support/MarioParty4/memcard-slot-a.raw` the player
uses.

## Run 1: a whole ten-turn board, eleven minigames, and a new crash

2026-09-13 into 2026-09-14, on the G4. **130,140 frames — 36 minutes of game
time — with four CPU players, unattended.** The filtered log is
`m7-soak-run1.log` (the raw 1 MB is mostly the game's own narration and was
not worth keeping).

It played a complete ten-turn game of Toad's Midway Madness and reached the
end-of-game results with `999/2c 999/1c 574/0c 994/2c` on the board. That is
the first time this port has finished a board, and it did it by itself.

### Every minigame it dealt

| module | entered | left |
|---|---:|---:|
| `m412dll` Snow Throw | 10,838 | 13,803 |
| `m428dll` The Rock Climb | 20,641 | 30,523 |
| `m403dll` Falling Wall | 34,943 | 36,151 |
| `m432dll` | 44,471 | 52,738 |
| `m416dll` Defend Fire | 59,762 | 62,452 |
| `m426dll` Kyorokyoro Panic | 69,584 | 72,207 |
| `m401dll` Waku-Guri Diving | 84,937 | 88,664 |
| `m424dll` Crane Catch | 95,450 | 99,097 |
| `m402dll` Big Slime | 106,585 | 109,567 |
| `m441dll` | 111,624 | 116,985 |
| `m429dll` Treasure Forest | 120,335 | 124,866 |

**Eleven distinct modules, entered and left without a crash.** Before M7 the
port was known to survive exactly two.

### Crashes

| frame | where | signal | address |
|---:|---|---|---|
| ~130,150 | `HuSprCall` <- `Hu3DExec`, in `mstory3dll` (the end-of-game results) | SIGSEGV (11) | `0x88888888` |

`HuSprCall` (`src/game/sprman.c:131`) dereferences
`sprite->data->bank[sprite->bank]`, so `sprite->data` was `0x88888888`.
**That value is not a fill pattern from anywhere in this tree** — `0x88` does
not appear as a memset or poison byte in `src/` or `port/` — so it is either
uninitialised memory or a stale sprite entry left in `HuSprOrder` by an
overlay that has been unloaded. The second is PLAN.md §4's risk 2, the
`dlclose` hazard, and it is testable directly: re-run with `--relzerobss`,
which zeroes a module's bss by hand on load, and with `--reldlclose`, which
really unloads. If the crash moves or disappears under either, that is the
answer.

`m425dll` did **not** come up in this run, so its SIGBUS (PLAN.md §17.7) is
not in this log.

### Ten stuck reports, all the same one

```
port> STUCK: frame 68297, 90 s with no scene change.  live screen w01dll
  (overlay 89, event 0, previous resultdll), turn 6/10, mg_next 65936
```

The board sits on `w01dll` for over 90 seconds between a minigame's results
and the next turn's roulette. That is the watchdog working as designed and
reporting something that is not a hang — a turn of four CPU players moving
around the board legitimately takes that long at 10 fps. **The threshold is
wrong, not the watch**: `--stuckwatch` should default to something like 240
for a board, or the watch should reset on `GWSystem.turn` as well as on the
overlay. Worth fixing before the next soak, because ten false positives make
a real one easy to miss.

`mg_next 65936` in those lines is the watchdog printing `GWSystem.mg_next`
between turns, when it holds no valid minigame. Cosmetic, and it should be
range-checked the way the status line's is.

### Cost over the whole run

`--status`' `aud` column, 2,169 samples — one a second for the entire soak:

| | mean | median | p95 | worst |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| M6 (§16.7, a 6,000-frame walk) | 1.61 | 1.72 | 3.13 | 5.08 |
| M7 soak run 1 (130,140 frames) | **2.21** | 2.25 | 3.17 | 4.02 |

**This is the honest number and it is worse.** PLAN.md §17.6 quoted 1.53 ms
from 119 seconds of a boot-and-menus segment and said at the time that it was
a comparison rather than a controlled A/B; over 36 minutes of real gameplay
the mean is 2.21 ms against a 1.5 ms budget. The tail is still better than
M6's (worst 4.02 against 5.08), which is consistent with the depop ramp doing
its job, but the 4-tap resampler looks like it costs more per sample than the
linear blend it replaced rather than less.

So the controlled A/B is no longer a nice-to-have: run the same command twice,
once with `--resample1`, and let the two `aud` columns decide. `--clickstat`
on both says whether the filter bought anything in exchange.

## Reading a soak log

| grep for | what you get |
|---|---|
| `enter minigame` / `left  minigame` | every m4xx module the soak played, with the frame |
| `STUCK` | the live screen, by name, when nothing has changed in the window |
| `port: fault` | the signal, the address, and the symbolised backtrace |
| `port> status` | one line a second; the `aud` column is the mixer's cost |
| `musyx_mix: --clickstat` | at shutdown: steps over half full scale, by `wavstat.py`'s own rule |
